WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Can two distant starting points give the same temperature trace?

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:17.000
On an insulated rod, pink and teal represent two alternative experiments.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:26.000
The heat fields differ, yet the midpoint traces coincide exactly.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:36.000
One pulse starts on the left, the other on the right. The record is identical.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:45.000
How could you locate the source from this record alone?

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Both displays start at the same positive time. No heat flows out at the ends.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:09.000
The gold probe reads the midpoint and carries that value onto the time trace.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Diffusion changes the distribution. The total heat in each experiment stays at 1.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:31.000
The heat fields remain reflections as time advances. The midpoint trace still coincides.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:41.000
Sample at the same eight times. Both sources give the same eight numbers.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Any number of midpoint readings still leaves the two sides indistinguishable.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:55.000
Reflection is the key. Cosine modes reveal exactly where the information disappears.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Reflect a to 1−a. Each cosine coefficient picks up a parity sign.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Odd modes flip and even modes stay. Only the odd terms can distinguish the sources.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:25.000
The field contributes through another cosine weight at the thermometer position.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Move the same probe to the midpoint. Every odd cosine has a zero there.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:55.000
The sensor zeros the odd terms, and the even terms already match. Equality holds for every positive time.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Fix diffusivity first. Changing strength A or position a changes the eight readings.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:14.000
The two directions are locally distinguishable. The unit contour shows curvature, rather than global uncertainty.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:24.000
At each candidate location, fit the best strength in [0.7, 1.3] and record the residual.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:36.000
At 0.23 the fit is exact. Keep scanning and test distant candidates against the same record.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:46.000
At 0.77 the residual returns to zero. Its own local curvature is positive too.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:55.000
With D added, Fisher remains full rank and the mirror identity still holds.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:05.000
Local precision describes nearby changes. These exact fits are 0.54 apart.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Move the thermometer while keeping the rod and both candidate sources fixed.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:27.000
Each pair of dots shows the two predictions at one sample time. At the center, all eight pairs coincide.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:41.000
Move to 0.495. Each gold segment marks the gap between two predictions for one reading.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:52.000
Combine all eight gaps and divide by the fixed noise scale to measure separation.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:05.000
At 0.49, theoretical error for the two known candidates drops from 50% to about 0.47%.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:20.000
This uses two fully specified, equally likely alternatives and independent Gaussian noise. Continuous localization asks more.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:30.000
Fix the probe at 0.49. What if all eight readings start later?

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:43.000
Shift the whole schedule together. The eight dots move along the same contrast curve.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:51.000
A delay of four model time units raises theoretical error to about 37.86%.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:06:05.000
At eight units, error is about 48.29%. Noise stays fixed while the physical signal weakens.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:15.000
High modes decay first, then even the slowest odd mode fades. Late readings lose location information.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:25.000
The study compares six layouts using the same budget: two sensors at four shared times.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:36.000
At zero delay and σ=0.05, each layout has zero large errors in 2,048 fits. Equal spacing has the lowest median.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:47.000
At delay four, every layout makes some large errors. These counts describe the synthetic benchmark.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Grid search can miss cases in between. Of the eight continuous enclosures, three lower bounds remain zero.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:09.000
A zero lower bound leaves the case unresolved. None of the eight proves an ideal estimator error bound below 5%.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:20.000
When designing an experiment, ask how precisely it estimates nearby changes and which distant explanations remain compatible.
